/* Built-in scripts: from Mariam Baruffwala's GEMP 1 Clinical Skills & OSCE Prep notes (2026). Converted line by line; nothing added. */
const BUILTIN = [{"name": "History taking (general)", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "History", "text": "# 1. Greet, ID & State Role\nHello.\nMy name is {me}, I am a Wits medical student.\nMay I please confirm your full name and age?\n# 2. Consent, Privacy & Autonomy\nLovely to meet you ____ .\nI have been asked to take your medical history.\nThis may involve some personal questions, but it is important that you answer as accurately as possible so that we can better help you.\nEverything said here will remain confidential, is it okay if I continue?\n# 3. Main Complaint (Open-Ended)\nHow can I help you today?\nCan you please tell me more about that?\n# 4. Secondary complaint\nIs there anything else you'd like to talk about?\n# 5. HPI Specifics: Pain / Symptom Profile\nSOCRATES\nWhere exactly is the pain? | S – Site\nCan you point to it with one finger?\nWhen did it start? | O – Onset\nDid it start suddenly or gradually?\nWhat triggered it?\nCan you describe the pain? | C – Character\nIs it sharp, dull, squeezing, burning, or crushing?\nDoes the pain travel anywhere else? | R – Radiation\nDo you experience anything else with the pain, like sweating, nausea, shortness of breath, or fever? | A – Associated Symptoms\nIs it constant, or does it come and go? | T – Timing\nHow long does it last?\nWhat makes it worse? | E – Exacerbating / Alleviating\nWhat makes it better, like resting or taking medications?\nOn a scale of 1 to 10, where 1 is barely felt and 10 is the worst pain of your life, how severe is it at its worst? | S – Severity\n# 6. Past Medical History\nNow, I would like to ask about your medical history.\nHave you been diagnosed with any chronic conditions?\nSuch as…\nDiabetes or High blood sugar\nEpilepsy\nAsthma / COPD\nTuberculosis\nHypertension\n[High blood]\n# 7. Medications, Admissions and Surgeries\nAre you on any medications?\n- Do you take these as prescribed?\nHave you been admitted into hospital before or underwent any surgeries?\n# 8. Family History & Contacts\nIn terms of your family history, do any of your parents suffer from chronic conditions or had any illnesses?\nIs anyone at home experiencing similar issues?\n# 9. Social History & Basic Amenities\nWhere do you live?\nDo you have access to clean running water, electricity, and flushing toilets at home?\nWho lives with you, and do you have a good support system?\nWhat do you do for work?\nDoes this involve heavy lifting or sitting for long periods of time?\nIs it stressful?\nDo you smoke cigarettes?\n[If yes: 'How many a day, and for how many years? Let's calculate your pack-years']\nDo you drink alcohol?\n[If yes, apply CAGE]\nHave you travelled recently, especially to a malaria-endemic area?\n# 10. General Health Screen (Systemic Review)\nI am going to run through a quick check of your general health using the\nSWEM GAB checklist:\nSleep: How is your sleep?\nWeight: Have you had any recent, unexplained weight loss?\nEnergy: How are your energy levels?\nMicturition: Any changes in urination?\nGynaecological:\n[For females: 'Are your periods regular?']\nAppetite: How is your appetite?\nBowel Habits: Any changes in your stools?\n# 11. Patient Perspective (RICE Mnemonic)\nTo make sure we address all your concerns today:\nReason/\nIdeas: What do you think is causing your symptoms today?\nConcerns: Is there anything specific you are worried this might be?\nExpectations: What are you hoping we can do for you today?\n# 12. Synthesis, Summary & Clinical Plan\nThank you, ____ .\nTo summarise, you came in with ____ .\nPhysically you feel__ | - Bio\nYou are worried about ____ . | - Psycho\nSocially, you need to manage work/family… | - Social\nMy main concern is that your symptoms point toward [Suspected Diagnosis].\nWe will investigate further and work together to help you.\nDo you have any questions for me?\nThank you so much, we are done.", "id": "b00", "builtin": true}, {"name": "History: CVS focused", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "History", "text": "# 1. Ischemic Pain Characterisation\nCan you describe the chest discomfort?\nDoes it feel like a tight, heavy, or squeezing band across your chest, or is it a sharp, stabbing pain?\nDoes the pain spread or travel anywhere else, such as up into your neck, your left jaw, your back, or down your left arm?\nDoes it worsen when you exert yourself physically, and is it relieved by resting?\nHave you ever taken a tablet or spray under your tongue for this, and did it help?\n# 2. Vegetative Symptoms Check\nWhen the chest discomfort is at its worst, do you feel sick to your stomach, or have you actually vomited?\nHave you noticed yourself sweating profusely, or feeling dizzy and lightheaded?\nDo you feel a sudden, intense sense of anxiety or dread?\n# 3. Left Heart Failure (LHF) Screen\nAre you experiencing shortness of breath?\nDoes the breathing difficulty get worse when you lie flat in bed, requiring you to prop yourself up with multiple pillows to sleep?\n[Orthopnoea]\nDo you ever wake up suddenly in the middle of the night gasping for air, forcing you to sit on the edge of the bed or run to an open window?\n[Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnoea - PND]\nDo you have a persistent cough?\nHave you noticed coughing up any pink, frothy fluid?\n# 4. Right Heart Failure (RHF) Screen\nHave you noticed swelling in both of your ankles?\nIf you press on the swelling, does it leave a dimple?\nDoes the swelling worsen as the day goes on and improve after lying down overnight?\nHave you noticed your abdomen becoming increasingly bloated, or do you feel a full, heavy sensation in your right upper stomach?\n# 5. Conduction & Arrhythmia Screen\nDo you ever feel your heart beating fast, fluttering, or pounding in your chest?\nDoes this sensation start and stop suddenly?\nCan you tap out the rhythm of this heartbeat on the table for me—is it fast and regular, or completely chaotic and irregular?\nHave you ever suddenly passed out, fainted, or felt like the room was spinning?\nDid you have any warning signs before passing out, like dizziness or sweating?\n# 6. Childhood Rheumatic Fever Screen\nAs a child, did you ever suffer from high fevers accompanied by painful, swollen joints that moved from one joint to another?\nDid you have any unusual skin rashes, lumps under your skin, or involuntary, jerky movements of your arms and legs?\n# 7. Infective Endocarditis (IE) Risk Screen\nHave you had a persistent, low-grade fever or night sweats over the last few weeks?\nHave you recently had any dental procedures or deep skin infections?\nDo you have a history of kidney dialysis, or do you use intravenous recreational drugs?\n# 8. CVS Specific Comorbidity Screen\nDo you have high blood pressure, high cholesterol, diabetes, or kidney disease?\nDoes anyone in your family have a history of early heart attacks, strokes, or sudden, unexplained deaths?", "id": "b01", "builtin": true}, {"name": "History: Resp focused", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "History", "text": "# 1. Cough Characterisation\nIs your cough dry or wet?\nIf it is wet, what color is the phlegm?\nIs it clear, white, yellow, green, or rusty-brown?\nHow much phlegm do you cough up—is it small teaspoons or large cups of sputum?\nIs it foul-smelling?\nHave you ever coughed up blood?\nWas it just streaks of blood mixed in with your phlegm, or was it pure, bright red blood?\n[Haemoptysis]\n# 2. Dyspnoea & Functional Class\nHow long have you been short of breath?\nDid it start suddenly\n[Within seconds to minutes]\n, or has it been getting progressively worse over weeks to months?\nHow far can you walk on flat ground before you have to stop to catch your breath?\nCan you climb a flight of stairs without stopping?\nCan you dress or wash yourself without struggling to breathe?\n# 3. Pleuritic Chest Pain Screen\nDo you experience chest pain when you breathe?\nIs it a sharp, stabbing pain that worsens when you take a deep breath in, cough, or sneeze?\nDoes the pain improve if you hold your breath or press firmly on that side of your chest?\n# 4. Wheeze & Atopic Triad Screen\nDo you hear a whistling or wheezing sound coming from your chest when you breathe out?\nIs this wheezing worse at night, in the early morning, or when you are exposed to cold air, dust, or animal fur?\nDo you have a personal or family history of asthma, dry itchy skin rashes\n[Eczema]\n, or frequent sneezing and runny nose\n[Hay fever]\n# 5. Standard Wits 4-Symptom TB Screen\nI am going to ask you four very important questions about tuberculosis\nHave you had a persistent cough for more than 2 weeks?\nHave you had drenching sweats at night that require you to change your clothes?\nHave you had fevers or felt hot and cold?\nHave you had any unexplained weight loss?\nHave you ever had TB before, or completed TB treatment?\nHas anyone in your home, workplace, or close circle been diagnosed with TB?\n# 6. Occupational & Hobby Risk Screen\nWhat do you do for a living, and what jobs have you done in the past?\nHave you ever worked underground in gold or coal mines, in construction, insulation, or dusty manufacturing factories?\nDo you keep any birds as pets, such as pigeons or budgies, or have hobbies involving bird feathers or droppings?", "id": "b02", "builtin": true}, {"name": "History: Renal & abdominal focused", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "History", "text": "# 1. Abdominal Pain Trajectory\nWhere did the pain first start?\nDid it start as a dull ache around your belly button and then move to become a sharp, constant pain in your right lower stomach?\n[Appendicitis]\nDoes the pain radiate straight through to your back, and is it partially relieved when you sit up and lean forward?\n[Pancreatitis]\nDoes the pain come in severe waves in your flank, traveling down toward your groin?\n[Renal Colic]\n# 2. GI Bleeding & Vomiting Screen\nHave you been vomiting?\nWhat does it look like?\nIs there any bright red blood, or dark, granular material that looks like coffee grounds?\n[Haematemesis]\nAre your stools normal brown?\nHave you noticed any bright red blood in your stools, or have they become black, tarry, sticky, and highly foul-smelling?\n[Melaena]\n# 3. Hepatobiliary & Portal Screen\nHave you noticed your eyes or skin turning yellow?\nDo you experience intense, generalized itching of your skin?\nHave you noticed your abdomen swelling or your legs becoming swollen and puffy?\nDo you have a history of heavy alcohol use or hepatitis infections?\n# 4. Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS)\nDo you experience a burning or stinging pain when you pass urine?\n[Dysuria]\nAre you passing urine much more frequently than usual?\nDo you have to wake up multiple times at night to empty your bladder?\n[Nocturia]\nDo you struggle to start your urine stream, have a weak or interrupted stream, or find yourself dribbling after you finish?\nDo you feel like your bladder is not completely empty after urinating?\n# 5. Haematuria Characterisation\nHave you noticed any blood in your urine?\nWhat color is it—is it bright red, pink, or a smoky-brown 'Coca-Cola' color?\nIs the bleeding completely painless, or is it accompanied by burning when you urinate or severe pain in your back?\n# 6. Constitutional Uremic Screen\nHave you been feeling extremely tired, weak, and continuously nauseous?\nHave you noticed a persistent, unpleasant metallic taste in your mouth?\nHave you been suffering from persistent hiccups or generalized itching?\nAre you passing very small amounts of urine, or has your urination stopped completely?", "id": "b03", "builtin": true}, {"name": "General examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & General Inspection\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI've been asked to perform a general physical exam.\nIs that okay?\nFirstly, I am observing that the patient appears comfortable at rest, is alert, and shows no signs of respiratory distress.\nThere is no obvious paraphernalia like oxygen or IV lines.\n# 2. Hands: Dorsal Aspect (Nails & Skin)\nPlease hold your hands out flat.\nI am inspecting the nails for spoon-shaping\n[Koilonychia]\n, splinter haemorrhages, or pallor.\nI am also checking the skin for scratch marks.\nNow, looking from the side, I am checking the\nLovibond angle\n[Nail-fold angle]\nPlease place your fingernails together like this\n[Schamroth's sign]\n# 3. Clubbing & Muscle Wasting\nI am palpating the nail bed for sponginess.\nI am also looking at the back of the hands for muscle wasting, particularly the interossei.\n# 4. Hydration (Skin Turgor)\nI am now gently pinching the skin on the back of your hand to check for your hydration levels.\nThere is immediate recoil.\n# 5. Hands: Palmar Aspect\nPlease turn your hands over.\nI am checking the palms for redness\n[Palmar erythema]\nor pallor.\nNow, I'll press on your fingernail for a moment to check your capillary refill time...\nit is under 2 seconds, which is normal.\n# 6. Vital Signs: Pulses\nI am feeling the radial pulse to check the rate and rhythm.\nNow, I'll feel both radial pulses to check for radio-radial delay.\nNow, I'll feel the carotid pulse in your neck—one side at a time—to check the volume and character.\nI would also check for radio-femoral delay.\n# 7. Face: Eyes\nPlease look up while I pull down your lower lids to check for conjunctival pallor.\nNow, look down while I lift your upper lids to check the sclera for yellowness\n[Jaundice]\nI am also looking for xanthelasma or arcus senilis.\n# 8. Mouth & Tongue\n[Use a LIGHT]\nPlease open your mouth.\nI am inspecting the lips for peripheral cyanosis or angular stomatitis.\nI see the mucosa is moist.\nPlease lift your tongue to check for central cyanosis and frenulum pallor.\nI'll also check your teeth and gums.\n# 9. Lymph Nodes (Cervical & Axillary)\nPlease sit up.\nI am palpating the cervical groups from behind:\nSubmental, submandibular, tonsillar, pre/post-auricular, occipital, posterior triangle,\nPlease look to the side\nI am feeling for the deep cervical LN along SCM\nRepeat on both sides and have pt look forwardand supraclavicular.\nFor the cervical lymph nodes, I did not feel any.\nIf I did, I would comment on their size, site, shape, consistency, tenderness and attachment.\nPlease remove your shirt\nI'm also checking the 5 axillary Lymph Nodes and I will need to feel your armpit.\nFeel for the epitrochlear LN\nFor the Axillary LN: - Apical\n- Anterior\n- Posterior\n- Lateral\n[Upwards on the arm]\nPress behind pec and feel for infraclavicular LN \"I did not feel any lymph nodes.\nI would then ask the patient to lie down and feel for their inguinal LN.\n# 10. Lower Body: Oedema\nPlease lie flat and roll up your pants.\nI'm inspecting for swelling.\nI'm pressing my thumb over the dorsum of your foot/bony prominence for about 5 - 10 seconds to check for swelling.\nI will also check behind the ankles medially.\n# 11. Severity/ Grading of Oedema\nAs oedema is present, I am moving up the shin to above the knee to measure the extent.\nI would check if it is unilateral or bilateral, pitting or non-pitting and look for sacral oedema.\nYou may put your shirt back on.\n# 12. Conclusion\nThank you, we are finished.\nI found/did not find ____ .\nWe will do some further investigations and see how best to help you.\nThank you.\n# Device\nFlow Rate\n# Nasal Cannula\n1–6 L/min\n# Simple Face Mask\n6–10 L/min\n# Partial Rebreather\n10–12 L/min\n# Non-Rebreather Mask\n10–15 L/min\n# Venturi Mask\nColor-coded", "id": "b04", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Blood pressure", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Introduction\nGood morning.\nMy name is {me}, and I am a third-year medical student.\n[While sanitising]\n# 2. Confirm Patient Details\nCould you please confirm your full name and age for me?\n# 3. Clean Hands\nBefore we begin, I am sanitising my hands to ensure the procedure is sterile and safe for both of us.\n# 4. Explain Procedure\nI have been asked to measure your blood pressure today.\nThis will involve me placing this fabric cuff around your arm, which will inflate and squeeze your arm gently.\nIt may feel tight or slightly uncomfortable for a few seconds, but it should not be painful.\nIs that alright with you?\n# 5. Informed Consent\nEverything we do today remains strictly confidential.\nAlso, please let me know if you feel any pain or want me to stop at any point.\nDo you have any questions before we start?\n# 6. Exclude Contraindications\nBefore I prepare the equipment, I need to ask: Do you have an arteriovenous fistula for dialysis in either arm?\nHave you ever had a mastectomy or lymph nodes removed from either side?\n# 7. Prepare Patient State\nThe patient appears calm and relaxed.\n[Ask if they have had caffeine, smoked, or climbed stairs in the last 30 minutes]\n# 8. Correct Seating Posture\nPlease sit back comfortably in your chair so your back is fully supported, and place both of your feet flat on the ground.\nDo not cross your legs.\n# 9. Arm Positioning & Exposure\nPlease roll up your sleeve so your arm is fully exposed, and rest your arm on this table for me so that it is level with your heart.\n# 10. Choose Correct Cuff Size\nI am selecting a cuff by measuring your mid-upper arm circumference\n[MUAC]\nThe bladder width of this cuff is 40% of your MUAC, and the bladder length is 80% of your MUAC, which is the correct size for your arm.\n# 11. Palpate Brachial Artery\nI am finding your biceps tendon in the cubital fossa, moving my fingers medially and superiorly to feel the pulse of your brachial artery.\n# 12. Place the Cuff\nI am wrapping this cuff around your arm, ensuring that the bladder arrow is aligned directly over your brachial artery, and positioning the lower edge of the cuff 2–3 cm above your elbow crease.\n# 13. Palpate the Radial Pulse\nI am palpating your radial pulse at your wrist using the pulps of my index and middle fingers.\n# 14. Estimate Systolic Pressure\nI am inflating the cuff while keeping my fingers on your radial pulse.\nThe pulse has disappeared at 120 mm\nHg.\nThis is my estimated systolic pressure.\n# 15. Deflate Fully\nI am deflating the cuff completely to allow normal blood flow to return to your arm.\n# 16. Stethoscope Placement\nI am placing the diaphragm of my stethoscope gently over the brachial pulse we located in your cubital fossa.\nI will ensure the chestpiece does not touch the cuff or its tubing.\n# 17. Inflate the Cuff\nI am inflating the cuff to 150 mm\nHg, which is 20 mm\nHg above my palpatory estimated systolic pressure.\n# 18. Deflate Slowly\nI am deflating the cuff slowly at a rate of 2 to 4 mm\nHg per second while listening carefully.\n# 19. Identify Systolic Point (KI)\nI hear the first clear, repetitive tapping sound at 122 mm\nHg.\nThis corresponds to Korotkoff Phase I\nand represents the Systolic Blood Pressure.\n# 20. Identify Diastolic Point (KV)\nThe sounds have completely disappeared at 82 mm\nHg.\nThis corresponds to Korotkoff Phase V\nand represents the Diastolic Blood Pressure.\n# 21. Complete Deflation\nI am letting the remaining air out of the cuff and removing it from your arm.\n# 22. Report Findings\n____ , your blood pressure reading today is 122 over 82 mm\nHg.\nThis is a normal, healthy reading.\n# 23. Address Target Ranges & Exceptions\nFor healthy individuals, we look for readings under 140 over 90 mm\nHg.\nHowever, if you had diabetes, we would target a lower threshold of 130 over 80 mm\nHg, whereas in pregnancy, normal baselines are naturally slightly lower.\n# 24. Explain Hypertension Diagnosis Rules\nPlease note that we cannot diagnose high blood pressure from a single reading today.\nA formal diagnosis of hypertension requires elevated readings on at least three separate occasions, taken a minimum of two days apart.\n# 25. Lifestyle Counseling\nTo help maintain a healthy blood pressure, it's always beneficial to follow a low-salt diet, exercise regularly, manage stress levels, and limit alcohol intake.\n# 26. Technical Closure\nI will now document these findings in your chart.\nThank you very much for your time.\nAre there any questions you have for me?\n[Final hand hygiene]\nThank you so much.", "id": "b05", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Venepuncture", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Positioning\n[Sanitize hands immediately upon entering]\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nNice to meet you.\nI've been asked to draw some blood for testing today.\nThis involves using a needle to take blood from your arm.\nYou will feel a sharp scratch as it goes in, but it shouldn't be too painful.\nPlease let me know if you feel extreme pain and I will stop.\nMay I proceed?\nPlease remove your jacket and rest your arm flat on this table.\n# 2. Preparation & Hand Hygiene\nI am now applying the tourniquet about four fingers above the elbow.\nIt will feel tight, but let me know if it's painful.\nPlease make a fist for me.\n[I will now wash my hands and don my gloves, and then feel for a spongy vein.]\n# 3. Cleaning the Site\nI am cleaning the area with an alcohol swab in small circular motions, radiating outwards.\nI will now let that dry.\nI am donning my gloves.\n# 4. Equipment Assembly\n[While alcohol dries]\nI am twisting the white cap off the needle and pushing the rubber-covered end into the vacutainer. | Vacutainer\nI am attaching the needle firmly to the syringe while keeping the cap on. | Syringe\n# 5. The Puncture\nI'm removing the needle cap now by pulling it straight out.\nI am anchoring the vein by pulling the skin down.\nSharp scratch coming now...\nI am inserting the needle bevel up at a 30°–45° angle.\n# 6. Drawing the Blood\nI'm stabilizing the vacutainer with my non-dominant hand and inserting the tube. | Vacutainer\nThe vacuum is drawing the blood naturally.\nI'm using my non-dominant hand to gently pull back the plunger while keeping the needle steady. | Syringe\n# 7. The \"1-2-3\" Removal\nI am releasing the tourniquet now.\nI'm placing this dry cotton wool over the site without pressing.\nPlease hold it gently.\nI'm withdrawing the needle smoothly.\nPlease apply firm pressure right here for me.\n# 8. Transfer & Disposal\nI am inserting the needle into the tube while it rests horizontally on the table.\nI am not pressing the plunger; I am letting the vacuum draw the blood.\nI am disposing of the needle and syringe into the sharps bin. | Disposal\n# 9. Labeling & Mixing\nI am inverting this tube 8 times gently.\nI'll now label the tube with your name, date of birth, and today's date at your bedside.\n# 10. Inspection & Closure\nI'm checking the site...\nthere is no further bleeding or swelling, so I'm securing the cotton wool with tape.\nI will then remove my gloves, dispose of them and sanitise my hands.\nThank you, we are finished.\nI'll send these to the lab with your blood form.\nResults usually take 4–6 hours.\nPlease alert us if you notice any pain or redness later.", "id": "b06", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Urine dipstick", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & MSU Instructions\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nI've been asked to perform a urine test.\nTo do this accurately, I need a midstream sample.\nPlease go to the bathroom and wipe the area first to prevent skin bacteria from contaminating the test.\nStart urinating into the toilet, then place the cup into the stream to collect the sample without stopping the flow, and finish the rest in the toilet.\n# 2. Preparation & Hand Hygiene\nWhile the patient is collecting the sample, I am washing my hands and donning gloves.\nI am gathering my equipment and checking the expiration date on the dipstick bottle to ensure the results are valid.\n# 3. Macroscopic Examination (Visual)\nI am starting with a visual inspection of the urine.\nColor: It is [specify color].\nClarity: I am checking for any sediment, debris, or clouds.\nFoam: I am looking for any persistent foam on the surface.\n# 4. Macroscopic Examination (Odor & Volume)\nNow I am checking the odor.\nRegarding volume, though it's an MSU sample, the patient produced approximately [amount] ml.\n# 5. Chemical Analysis: The Dip\nI am removing a single strip without touching the colored blocks and closing the container immediately.\nI am submerging the stick in the sample for 10 seconds, ensuring all blocks are covered.\nI am removing the stick, tapping the edge to remove excess urine, and placing it horizontally on the container lid to start my timer.\n# 6. Interpretation & Timing\nI am interpreting the results from top to bottom by comparing the strip to the key on the bottle.\nI am leaving the leukocytes for last, as they require 60–90 seconds to fully develop.\n# 7. Key Interpretation Findings\nMy findings are: [Report each result].\nNitrites/Leukocytes: [Positive/Negative]\nGlucose/Ketones: [Positive/Negative]\nProtein/Blood: [Positive/Negative]\n# 8. Completion & Disposal\nI am now discarding the dipstick into the red biohazard bin.\nI will discard the urine in the toilet, remove my gloves, and wash my hands again.\n# 9. Findings & Further Tests\nThank you, Mr.\nDlamini.\nBased on the dipstick showing [findings], I would like to order a\nUrine Microscopy, Culture and Sensitivity\n[MC&S]\nto identify specific bacteria, and possibly a 24-hour urine collection for protein quantification.", "id": "b07", "builtin": true}, {"name": "IV cannulation", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Positioning\nImmediately wash hands\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI've been asked to perform an IV cannulation today.\nThis involves placing a small tube into your vein so we can give you fluids or medication.\nYou will feel a sharp scratch as the needle goes in, but it shouldn't be too painful.\nMay I proceed?\nPlease roll up your sleeve, rest your arm on the table, and make a fist for me.\n# 2. Fluid & Heplock Preparation\nI am checking the fluid name and expiry date.\nI'm ensuring the administration set is connected, the drip chamber is partially filled, and the line is flushed of all bubbles.\nI am also priming my short line: I've drawn 5ml of saline, flushed the short line until bubble-free, and left the syringe attached and accessible.\n# 3. Site Selection & Hygiene\nI am applying the tourniquet 10cm proximal to the site.\n[I am sanitizing my hands and donning gloves.]\nI am palpating for a spongy vein, ideally the median cubital.\nNow, I'm cleaning the area with an alcohol swab in circular motions, radiating outwards, and allowing it to dry for 30 seconds.\n# 4. The Insertion\nI'm opening the cannula and inspecting it.\nI am anchoring the vein distally.\nSharp scratch coming now...\nI am inserting the needle bevel up at a 10°–30° angle.\n# 5. Flashback & Advancement\nI have flashback in the chamber.\nI am advancing the needle/cannula another 1mm into the skin.\nNow, holding the needle steady with my dominant hand, I am using my non-dominant hand to advance the catheter only all the way into the vein.\n# 6. The \"1-2-3\" Needle Removal\nI am releasing the tourniquet.\nI am occluding the vein proximal to the catheter tip to prevent bleeding.\nI am withdrawing the needle and disposing of it in the sharps bin immediately.\n# 7. Connection & Flushing\nI am now attaching the primed short line\n[Or fluid set]\nto the catheter.\nI'll ask you to help hold the tube steady while I secure it with this transparent dressing.\nNow, I am flushing the line with saline...\nthere is no resistance or swelling, so the placement is correct.\n# 8. Waste & Safety Netting\nI am disposing of paper waste in the open bin and soiled items in the red biohazard bin.\nThank you, we are done.\nPlease alert us immediately if you notice any redness, swelling, or pain at this site.\n# 9. Removal of IV Cannula\nI'm going to remove the drip now.\n[I've washed my hands and put on gloves.]\nI've stopped the fluids.\nI'm removing the dressing—this might sting slightly.\nPlease hold this dry cotton wool gently over the site.\nI am withdrawing the catheter smoothly.\nNow, please apply firm pressure here.\nI am disposing of the cannula in the sharps bin.\n# 10. Inspection & Tape\nI'm checking the site after 5 minutes...\nthere is no further bleeding, so I am securing the cotton wool with tape.\nThank you very much.", "id": "b08", "builtin": true}, {"name": "IM injection", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "BCMH", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Screening\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI've been asked to give you an injection into your muscle.\nThis is for [medication name].\nIt may be a bit sore, but it will be over quickly.\nDo you have any allergies?\nMay I proceed?\n# 2. Site Evaluation & Positioning\nI am choosing the [site: Deltoid/Ventrogluteal/Vastus Lateralis].\nI am inspecting the skin for any signs of infection or bruising.\nPlease relax the muscle as much as possible.\n# 3. PPE & Preparation\nI am now putting on my apron, sanitizing my hands, and donning gloves.\nI am checking the medication name, dose, and expiration date against the prescription.\n# 4. Drawing Medication\nI am using a drawing needle to draw the medication from the container.\nI am holding the vial downwards and avoiding the last drops to prevent bubbles.\nI'm inserting the needle bevel up to prevent dulling.\nI will dispose of it in the sharps bin, and then attach the injection needle.\n# 5. Cleaning the Site\nI am cleaning the injection site with an alcohol swab in circular motions, radiating outwards.\nI will allow 30 seconds for this to dry completely.\n# 6. Z-Track Technique & Insertion\nI am using the\nZ-track technique by laterally displacing the skin.\nI'm inserting the needle at a 75° to 90° angle with a quick, darting movement.\n# 7. Aspiration & Injection\nI am aspirating for 5–10 seconds\n[Crucial for dorsogluteal]\nThere is no blood, so I am not in a vessel.\nI am injecting slowly at 10 seconds per ml to allow the muscle fibers to stretch.\n# 8. Wait, Withdraw & Dispose\nI am waiting 10 seconds for the drug to diffuse.\nI'm now withdrawing the needle with a small, steady movement.\nI am disposing of the needle into the sharps bin immediately using the scoop method if recapping is required.\n# 9. Site Assessment & Pain Relief\nI'm checking the site for any immediate bleeding or swelling.\nIf you feel pain, please cough vigorously to help distract the nervous system.\n# 10. Conclusion & Documentation\nThank you, we are finished.\nIt is normal for the site to be sore.\nHowever, if the pain worsens after 48 hours, or you notice redness or pus, please seek medical care.\nI am now disposing of my PPE, washing my hands, and documenting the procedure.", "id": "b09", "builtin": true}, {"name": "CVS general examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "CVS", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Positioning\n[Wash hands when you enter]\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nI have been asked to perform an examination with a focus on your heart.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nFor this procedure, I will need to observe you and possibly feel your hands, face, feet, and chest.\nIs that okay?\nThank you.\nPlease remove your shirt.\nI'm positioning the bed at a 45° angle; you may lie down.\n# 2. General Inspection\nThe patient appears comfortable at rest, alert, and is not in respiratory distress.\nLooking at the habitus, there are no signs of wasting or obesity.\nI note no dysmorphic features suggestive of\nMarfan or\nDown syndrome.\nThere is no paraphernalia such as oxygen masks, ECG leads, or defibrillators.\nI would then proceed to check the pt vitals\n# 3. Pulses (Radial & Carotid)\nI am palpating the radial pulse for rate and rhythm.\nWhile I do this, I am also observing the respiratory rate.\nNow, I'm feeling the carotid pulse for volume and character.\nI'll check both radial pulses for radio-radial delay.\nNow, I'll palpate the radial and femoral pulses simultaneously...\nI feel the femoral pulse before or with the radial, so there is no radio-femoral delay.\n# 4. Collapsing Pulse & BP\nDo you have any pain in your shoulder?\nNo?\nI am lifting your arm to check for a collapsing pulse...\nI do not feel a forceful, bounding pulse.\nAt this point, I would also measure the patient's blood pressure and temperature.\n# 5. Hands: Palmar Aspect\nPlease hold your hands up, palms facing me.\nThe palms are pink, well-perfused, and of normal temperature with no sweating.\nThere are no\nJaneway lesions or\nOsler's nodes.\nCapillary refill time is under 2 seconds.\nThere are no tar stains.\n# 6. Hands: Dorsal (Nails & Tendons)\nPlease turn your hands over.\nThere is no muscle wasting or signs of dehydration.\nLooking from the side, I'm checking for clubbing.\nPlease place your nails together; I see a clear\nSchamroth's window.\nI'm checking the tendons on the back of the hand for xanthomata.\n# 7. Face & Eyes\nPlease look up while I pull your lower lids to check for conjunctival pallor.\nNow, please look down while I lift the upper lids to check the sclera for jaundice.\nI am looking for fatty deposits around the eyes\n[Xanthelasma]\nor a grey ring around the iris\n[Arcus senilis]\nThe cheeks do not show mitral facies.\n# 8. Mouth & Tongue\nPlease open your mouth and lift your tongue.\nI see a pink tongue and frenulum with no central cyanosis.\nMucous membranes are moist.\nThere are no ulcers or candidiasis.\nDentition is healthy with no carriers.\nI see a normal, not high-arched, palate.\n# 9. Lower Limbs\nI'm inspecting the feet for swelling.\nI'm pressing firmly over the dorsum of the feet and the medial malleolus for 30 seconds to check for pitting oedema.\nI would then proceed up the shin to above the knee to measure severity.\n# 10. Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP)\nPlease turn your head slightly to the left.\nI am observing pulsations between the heads of the sternocleidomastoid muscle.\nThe pulsations are not palpable or occludable.\nI'm placing one ruler horizontally at the top of the pulse and another vertically at the\nAngle of Louis.\nThe measurement is [x]cm.\nI am adding 5cm to this value to account for the depth of the right atrium.\n# 11. Abdominojugular Reflex\n[If JVP is high/borderline]\n: \"I am going to press on your abdomen now; it may be slightly uncomfortable.\nI'm applying pressure for 15–30 seconds while watching the JVP...\nI see a 1cm rise that is not sustained, which is normal.\n# 12. Conclusion\nThank you, we are finished.\nYou may put your shirt back on.\nDo you have any questions?\n[Wash hands again]", "id": "b10", "builtin": true}, {"name": "CVS precordium & auscultation", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "CVS", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nI've been asked to perform a heart exam, which involves feeling and listening to your chest.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nIs it okay if I continue?\n[I am now sanitizing my hands and cleaning my stethoscope.]\n# 2. Positioning & Exposure\nPlease remove your shirt.\nI'm positioning the bed at a 45° angle.\nI am approaching you from your right side.\n# 3. Precordium Inspection\nI'm inspecting the chest from the front and side.\nIt is symmetrical.\nI see no surgical scars, such as a median sternotomy\n[Cardiac surgery]\n, lateral thoracotomy\n[Lung surgery]\n, or infraclavicular\n[Pacemaker]\nThere are no visible pulsations, foreign bodies\n[Pacemakers]\n, or shape abnormalities like barrel chest or pectus excavatum.\n# 4. Palpation: Apex Beat\nI'm finding the sternal angle to locate the 2nd intercostal space\n[ICS]\nand counting down to the 5th ICS.\nStarting from the mid-axillary line, I'm moving medially.\nI feel the apex beat at the 5th ICS, mid-clavicular line.\nIt is well-localized, non-forceful, and non-tapping.\n# 5. Palpation: Heaves & Thrills\nI'm placing the heel of my hand left parasternally to check for a parasternal heave.\nNow, I'm using my palm to feel for thrills\n[Palpable murmurs]\nover the mitral, tricuspid, aortic, and pulmonary areas.\nFinally, I'm feeling over the pulmonary valve\n[2nd ICS left]\nfor a palpable P2.\n# 6. Auscultation (Order: MATP)\nI am listening to the four valve areas.\nI'm feeling your carotid pulse simultaneously to identify S1.\nMitral: 5th ICS, MCL.\nAortic: 2nd ICS, right sternal border.\nTricuspid: 4th ICS, left sternal border.\nPulmonary: 2nd ICS, left sternal border.\n# 7. Heart Sounds & Splitting\nI hear normal intensity first and second heart sounds with no additional sounds or murmurs.\nPlease take a deep breath in and out...\nI hear physiological splitting of S2 on inspiration over the pulmonary area, which is normal.\n# 8. Dynamic Maneuvers\nPlease turn onto your left side.\nI'm using the bell at the apex to listen for the mid-diastolic murmur of\nMitral Stenosis.\nNow, please sit up and lean forward, breathe out and hold it.\nI'm using the diaphragm at the 2nd ICS left to listen for the early diastolic murmur of\nAortic Regurgitation.\n# 9. Posterior Chest Exam\nWhile you're sitting up, I'm inspecting your back.\nPlease take deep breaths through your mouth.\nI hear no bronchial breathing or crackles, which excludes pulmonary oedema.\nI see no signs of presacral pitting oedema.\n# 10. Abdominal Review\nPlease lie flat again.\nI'm palpating for hepatomegaly on the right and splenomegaly on the left.\nI am percussing across the abdomen to check for dullness\n[Ascites]\n# 11. Conclusion & Investigations\nThank you, we are finished.\nYou may put your shirt on.", "id": "b11", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Resp general examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "Resp", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Positioning\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nNice to meet you.\nI've been asked to perform a respiratory examination, which involves looking at your breathing system.\nI will need to look at and feel your hands, face, neck, and chest.\nIt shouldn't be painful, but let me know if you feel uncomfortable.\nMay I continue?\nPlease remove your shirt; I'm positioning the bed at a 45° angle.\nPlease lie down.\n[Wash hands]\n# 2. Initial Observation & Distress\nFrom talking to the patient, I didn't ear any hoarseness.\nI note no signs of respiratory distress: no pursed-lip breathing, no accessory muscle use, and the patient is not in a tripod position.\nThere is no audible coughing, wheezing, or stridor.\nThe patient appears comfortable on room air with no oxygen supplementation or other paraphernalia.\n# 3. Hands: Clubbing & Staining\nPlease hold your hands out, palms down.\nI'm looking at the nails from the top and side for clubbing and feeling for sponginess of the nail bed.\nPlease put your fingernails together like this...\nI see a clear\nSchamroth's window.\nThere is no tar staining on the fingers.\n# 4. Hands: Muscles & Wrists\nPlease spread your fingers and don't let me push them together.\nI'm checking for intrinsic muscle weakness.\nI note no muscle wasting.\nNow, I'm feeling your wrists for any tenderness...\nthere is none, which excludes\nHPO.\n# 5. Flapping Tremor\nPlease hold your hands straight out, cock your wrists back, and close your eyes.\nI'm observing for a flapping tremor\n[Asterixis]\nfor 15 seconds...\nI see no flap.\n# 6. Face: Sinuses & Nose\nI'm going to touch your face now…I'm palpating your sinuses for any [tenderness]\n[Http://tenderness.is]\n, let me know if there is any pain...\nNow, using my torch, I'm inspecting the nose for a deviated septum, polyps, or signs of inflammation.\n# 7. Face: Eyes & Mouth\nPlease look up for me [check conjunctiva] and look down [check sclera].\nI note no conjunctival pallor or jaundice.\nPlease open your mouth and lift your tongue.\nThe mucous membranes are moist and pink.\nI see no central cyanosis and no inflammation of the pharynx or tonsils.\n# 8. Horner Syndrome Screening\nLooking at the eyes, I note no ptosis\n[Drooping]\n, miosis\n[Constricted pupil]\n, or anhidrosis\n[Lack of sweating]\non one side of the face.\nThis makes an apical Pancoast tumor less likely.\n# 9. Pemberton's Sign\nPlease lift your arms straight up over your head for one minute.\nI am looking for facial plethora, cyanosis, or an inspiratory stridor...\nthere is no evidence of\nSVC obstruction.\n# 10. Neck & Trachea\nI'm feeling for your lymph nodes…\nAnd the position of your trachea.\nIt is central.\nI note no tracheal tug during breathing.\n# 11. Lower Limbs\nI'm inspecting your legs for any swelling.\nI am pressing over the shin for 30 seconds to check for pitting oedema.\nI am also looking for signs of a\nDVT, such as calf redness or warmth.\n# 12. Conclusion\nThank you very much, you can put your shirt back on.\n[Wash hands again]\nThe examination was normal.\nDo you have any questions?\nWe are done.", "id": "b12", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Resp focused examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "Resp", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI've been asked to perform a chest exam, which involves looking at, feeling, and listening to your chest.\nIt shouldn't be painful, but let me know if you feel uncomfortable.\nMay I proceed?\nPlease remove your shirt and sit upright for me.\n# 2. Inspection (Shape & Skin)\nThe shape of the chest is normal from the front and side.\nI note no barrel chest, pigeon chest, or pectus excavatum.\nThe skin appears normal with no erythema, bruising, or surgical scars.\nI am also looking for\nHarrison's sulcus or\nHoover's sign- Please breathe in and out.\nThere is no paradoxical chest rise, excluding Hoover's sign.\n# 3. Inspection (Symmetry)\nI am standing behind you now to observe your shoulders.\nPlease take a deep breath in and out...\nanother one.\nThere is symmetrical chest rise both anteriorly and posteriorly.\n# 4. Palpation (General)\nI am feeling down your chest wall now.\nLet me know if you feel any pain.\nI am checking for tenderness or subcutaneous emphysema.\nI am placing my palms on the chest wall, please say \"99\".\nI'm checking for equal vibrations bilaterally to check vocal fremitus.\n[Repeat posteriorly]\n# 5. Palpation (Apex Beat)\nI'm locating your sternal angle to find the 5th intercostal space.\nStarting from the mid-axillary line, I'm moving medially.\nI feel the apex beat at the 5th ICS, mid-clavicular line.\nit is well-localized and not forceful or tapping.\n# 6. Palpation (Expansion)\nI'm placing my hands with fingertips at your axillae and my thumbs meeting in the midline, slightly off the chest.\nTake a deep breath in...\nthe thumbs separated by more than 5cm, which is normal and symmetrical.\n# 7. Percussion (Anterior)\nI'm percussing from above the clavicles down the chest wall.\nIt is resonant bilaterally.\nI note expected dullness in the right lower zone where the liver sits.\n# 8. Auscultation (Breath Sounds)\nPlease look to the left and take deep breaths through your mouth.\nI'm listening at the clavicles with the bell first, then moving down with the diaphragm.\nI hear vesicular breath sounds of equal intensity with no added sounds like wheezing or crackles.\n# 9. Vocal Resonance\nEvery time I place my stethoscope, please say '99'.\nThe sounds are muffled and equal bilaterally, which is normal.\n# 10. Posterior Chest Exam\nPlease sit up and cross your arms over your chest to move your shoulder blades.\nI'm repeating the palpation, percussion, and auscultation on your back...\nfindings are normal and symmetrical.\n# 11. Conclusion\nThank you, {me}.\nWe are finished.\nYou may put your shirt back on.\nEverything appears normal.\nDo you have any questions?\n[I am now cleaning my hands.]\n# System/Step\nAbnormal Finding\n# General\nHoarseness\n# Hands\nClubbing\n# Face\nHorner's Syndrome\n# Neck\nTracheal Deviation\n# Chest Palpation\nSubcutaneous Emphysema\n# Percussion\nStony Dull\n# Auscultation\nBronchial Breathing", "id": "b13", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Chest X-ray", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "Resp", "text": "# 1. Identification & Labels\nI am starting by confirming the patient's name and age.\nThe date and time of the radiograph are [Date/Time].\nThis is a Chest X-ray.\nI note the Left/Right labels are correct as the gastric air bubble is seen on the left.\n# 2. Image Quality: Projection & Position\nThis is a\nPA film because the scapulae are retracted from the lung fields and the clavicles are in a\nV-shape.\nThe patient was in an erect position at the time of the film, indicated by the gastric air bubble.\n# 3. Image Quality: RIPE Criteria\nRotation: The vertebral spinous processes are central between the clavicular heads.\nInspiration: The anterior end of the 6th rib crosses the mid-diaphragm, indicating adequate inflation.\nPenetration: I can just barely distinguish the vertebrae through the heart shadow, so the exposure is adequate.\n# 4. Soft Tissues & Bones\nI am inspecting the soft tissues for any masses, surgical emphysema, or breast tissue.\nLooking at the bones, I am checking the ribs, clavicles, and vertebrae for any fractures, tumors, or signs of arthritis/osteoporosis.\n# 5. A - Airways & Mediastinum\nThe trachea is central at the level of the manubrium and deviates slightly to the right at the aortic knuckle.\nThe mediastinum is not widened.\nThe left hilum is slightly higher than the right, which is normal.\n# 6. B - Breathing (Lung Fields)\nI am dividing the lung fields into upper, middle, and lower zones.\nThey appear symmetrical in density.\nThe peripheral vessels are visible and normally distributed to the pleura.\nThere are no opacities, effusions, or reticular markings.\n# 7. C - Circulation (Heart)\nThe heart is normally positioned.\nI am estimating the\nCardio-Thoracic Ratio\n[CTR]\n, which is less than 50%, indicating a normal heart size.\nThe heart borders are sharp.\n# 8. D - Diaphragm\nThe diaphragms are dome-shaped.\nThe right hemidiaphragm is slightly higher than the left.\nThe costophrenic angles are acute and sharply defined.\nI see no free air under the diaphragm.\n# 9. E - Extras (Foreign Bodies)\nI am checking for any foreign bodies such as ECG leads, pacemakers, chest drains, or tubes\n[NGT/ETT]\n# 10. Conclusion & Summary\nIn summary, this is an adequately inspired PA chest radiograph of [Patient Name] showing [Pathology Findings/Normal Film].", "id": "b14", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Abdominal general examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "Renal", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI've been asked to perform an abdominal exam, which involves looking at and feeling your body from head to toe, focusing on your stomach.\nIt shouldn't be painful; let me know if you feel uncomfortable.\nMay I proceed?\nPlease remove your shirt and lie flat.\n# 2. General Inspection\nThe patient appears [well/ill] and alert.\nI note no signs of wasting, which might indicate chronic illness or GI malignancy.\nThe patient is not clutching their abdomen in pain\n[Acute abdomen]\nThere is no obvious distension.\nI see no paraphernalia like stoma bags, NG tubes, or drains.\n# 3. Hands: Nails & Palms\nPlease hold your hands out.\nLooking at the nails, there is no leukonychia\n[Hypoalbuminemia/liver failure]\nor koilonychia\n[Iron deficiency]\nNo clubbing.\nThe palms show no palmar erythema\n[Liver disease]\nor Dupuytren's contracture.\nCapillary refill is under 2 seconds, and skin turgor is normal.\n# 4. Hepatic Flap (Asterixis)\nPlease stretch your arms out, separate your fingers, and cock your wrists back.\nHold that for 15 seconds...\nI see no jerky, irregular 'bird's wing' movements.\n# 5. Arms & Axilla\nI'm inspecting the arms for bruising or petechiae, which might indicate clotting abnormalities or severe liver disease.\nThere are no spider naevi or scratch marks\n[Pruritus]\nI'm checking the axillae for acanthosis nigricans.\n# 6. Eyes & Face\nPlease look up while I pull your lower lids\n[Check conjunctival pallor]\nNow look down while I lift the upper lids\n[Check scleral jaundice]\nI note no xanthelasma or\nKayser-Fleischer rings in the cornea.\n# 7. Mouth & Tongue\nPlease open your mouth and lift your tongue.\nI see no angular stomatitis or ulcers.\nDentition is healthy.\nThe tongue is not smooth or inflamed\n[Atrophic glossitis]\nThere is no halitosis\n[Foul breath]\n# 8. Neck: Lymph Nodes\nI'm palpating the cervical and supraclavicular lymph nodes.\nI note no enlargement.\nSpecifically, I am feeling for\nVirchow's node in the left supraclavicular fossa.\n# 9. Chest (Male)\nLooking at the chest, I note no gynecomastia or spider naevi or hair loss.\n# 10. Lower legs\nFinally looking at the lower legs, I am not seeing any oedema\n[Check]\n, signs of pruritis, skin changes or clubbing on the nails\n# 11. Thank you and close\nThank you so much.\nWe are done with the procedure, I did not find anything abnormal.\nAny questions?\nYou may get dressed.\n[Wash hands]", "id": "b15", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Abdominal focused examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "Renal", "text": "# 1. Intro,\n[Wash hands]\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\n# 2. Consent & Prep\nI've been asked to perform an abdominal exam, which involves looking at, feeling, and listening to your stomach area.\nIt shouldn't be painful, but let me know if it's uncomfortable.\nMay I proceed?\nPlease remove your shirt and lie flat for me.\n# 3. Abdominal Inspection\nI'm inspecting the abdomen from the front and side.\nThe shape is normal with no fullness of the flanks.\nThere are no visible pulsations, distended veins, or surgical scars.\nI note no obvious protruding masses or general distension.\nI don't see any discolouration, striae or bruising.\nIf it were distended, I would consider the 6 Fs: Fluid, Fat, Flatulence, Faeces, Fulminant masses or Fetus.\n[Look from the side]\nPlease take slow deep breaths.\nNormal shape and symmetry…\n# 4. Palpation (9 Regions)\nPlease bend your knees and open your mouth slightly to relax the muscles.\nIs there any pain anywhere?\nIs there pain anywhere?\nI will palpate their last.\nI'm starting with superficial palpation, or feeling, away from the pain...\nnow moving to deep palpation.\nI'm looking at your face for any signs of tenderness.\nThe abdomen is soft and non-tender with no superficial or deep masses.\n# 5. Percussion & Auscultation\nI'm percussing the 9 regions; it is resonant throughout.\nNow, I'm listening for bowel sounds below and to the left of the umbilicus...\nbowel sounds are present.\n# 6. Liver Assessment\nPlease take normal breaths.\nI'm palpating from the right iliac fossa toward the costal margin, moving my hand only during expiration and keeping it still during inspiration.\nNo liver edge is felt.\nI'm percussing for the upper border at the 5th/6th ICS and the lower border at the costal margin.\nThe liver span is 10cm, which is normal\n[Under 13cm]\n# 7. Spleen & Kidney Assessment\nI'm placing my left hand behind your back and palpating diagonally from the RIF toward the left costal margin.\nNo spleen edge is felt.\nI'm percussing\nTraube's space\n[Between 11th/12th rib]\n; it is resonant on inspiration, excluding splenomegaly.\nNow, I'm balloting the kidneys...\nthey are not palpable.\nPlease sit up for\nMurphy's punch...\nthere is no renal angle tenderness.\n# 8. Ascites: Shifting Dullness\nPlease lie flat.\nI'm percussing from the midline toward the flanks.\nI note no change from resonance to dullness.\n[If dullness was noted]\n: Please turn toward me and wait 90 seconds.\nPercussing the same area...\nthe dullness has shifted, confirming shifting dullness.\n# 9. Ascites: Fluid Thrill\nPlease place the edge of your hand on the midline of your stomach.\nI'm placing my left hand on your flank and flicking the opposite side with my right hand...\nI feel no fluid thrill.\n# 10. Conclusion\nThank you, we are finished.\nYou may put your shirt back on.\nThe examination was normal.\nDo you have any questions?\n[I am now washing my hands again.]", "id": "b16", "builtin": true}, {"name": "Digital rectal examination", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "GIT", "text": "# 1. Intro, Consent & Chaperone\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI have been asked to perform a digital rectal exam.\nThis involves looking between your buttocks and using a finger to feel inside.\nIt is important to guide your treatment.\nI understand it is uncomfortable; would you like a chaperone present?\nIs it okay if I continue?\n# 2. Positioning\nYou may choose one of three positions: lying on your back with legs up, standing and leaning over the bed, or lying on your left side with your knees pulled to your chest.\nPlease remove your pants and underpants and cover yourself with this sheet.\nI'll be back in a moment.\n# 3. Prep & Equipment\n[Verbalize while preparing]\n: \"I am gathering my equipment: a towel, lubricant, cotton wool, gauze, and an apron.\n[I am now washing my hands and putting on gloves.]\n# 4. External Inspection\nI am uncovering you now.\nI'm inspecting for the 8 common pathologies: fissures, fistulae, pruritus, prolapse, haemorrhoids, skin tags, warts, or malignancies.\nI see none.\nPlease strain gently...\nI note no abnormal descent, patulous anus, or incontinence.\n# 5. Surface Sensation\nI'm touching the four quadrants around the anus with this cotton pad...\nI note a normal anal wink contraction.\n# 6. Insertion & Tone\nI'm lubricating my finger.\nI'm going to insert it now; please let me know if you feel any pain.\nI'm noting the anal tone...\nit is normal.\n# 7. Prostate Assessment (Male)\nI am palpating the anterior wall.\nThe prostate is walnut-sized and symmetrical with a palpable central sulcus.\nIt feels smooth and rubbery.\n# 8. 360° Sweep & Function\nI'm performing a 360° sweep to check for internal lesions.\nPlease strain against my finger...\nnow please squeeze my finger as hard as you can.\n# 9. Posterior Palpation\nI'm palpating the puborectalis posteriorly.\nDo you feel any pain?\nThere is no tenderness or inflammation noted.\n# 10. Withdrawal & Stool Exam\nI am removing my finger now.\nI am inspecting the glove for stool, blood, mucus, or pus...\nthe glove is clean.\n# 11. Conclusion & Cleanup\nThank you for your cooperation.\nI know that was uncomfortable, but I did not find anything abnormal.\nYou may get dressed.\nDo you have any questions?\nI am disposing of my gloves and performing a final hand wash.\n# Case / Differential\nHistory & Examination Indications\n# Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) vs. Malignancy\nHistory: Hesitancy, dribbling, poor stream, nocturia, and urgency.\nExam: Distended bladder or hematuria\n[Red/pink urine]\non dipstick.\n# Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding (UGIB)\nHistory: Haematemesis\n[Vomiting blood]\n, dizziness, syncope, or a history of heavy NSAID/alcohol use.\nExam: Tachycardia, hypotension, or epigastric tenderness.\n# Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding (LGIB) / Malignancy\nHistory: Weight loss, altered bowel habits, and fresh red blood mixed with stool.\nExam: Palpable abdominal mass or Virchow's node\n[Troisier's sign]\n# Acute Anorectal Pathology\nHistory: Severe pain during defecation, perianal itching\n[Pruritus]\n, or a \"lump\" felt by the patient.\n# Bowel Obstruction / Faecal Impaction\nHistory: Absolute constipation, colicky pain, and vomiting\n[Potentially faeculent]\nExam: Distended abdomen\n[One of the 6 Fs]\nand \"Coffee Bean\" sign on AXR.\n# Neurological / Sacral Root Injury\nHistory: History of trauma, sudden-onset faecal incontinence, or \"saddle anesthesia\".\nSteppage gait\" or other motor/sensory deficits in lower limbs. | Exam", "id": "b17", "builtin": true}, {"name": "NG tube insertion", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "GIT", "text": "# 1. Intro & Consent\nGood day, my name is {me} and I'm a third-year medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI need to insert a small tube through your nose into your stomach to [state indication, e.\n, help drain your stomach/start feeds].\nIt is not painful but will be uncomfortable.\nYou will need to take small sips of water to help it pass.\nIs it okay if I proceed?\n# 2. Screen for Contraindications\n[Ask patient]\n: \"Have you had any recent facial injuries, nose surgeries, or a history of oesophageal varices?\n[Assess patient]\n: \"I am checking for signs of a base-of-skull fracture, such as raccoon eyes or bruising behind the ears\n[Battle sign]\n, CSF rhinorrhoea or otorrhoea or haemotympanium).\n# 3. Positioning & Preparation\nI'm positioning you at a 45° angle with your neck slightly flexed.\nPlease place this linen saver on your chest and hold this glass of water and kidney dish.\n[I am now sanitizing my hands and putting on gloves.]\n# 4. Measurement\nI am measuring the tube from the tip of your nose to your earlobe, and then down to the xiphisternum.\nI'm noting this length [x cm] using the markings on the tube.\n# 5. Insertion Technique\nI'm lubricating the tip.\nPlease look straight ahead.\nI am inserting the tube into your larger nostril and advancing it along the floor of the nose.\nYou may feel resistance; please take sips of water now...\nswallow, swallow...\n# 6. Monitor for Misplacement\nI am monitoring the patient's breathing.\nThere is no coughing, choking, or cyanosis, and the patient can still speak, making tracheal placement unlikely.\n# 7. Securement\nI have reached the pre-measured mark.\nI am now strapping the tube securely to the nose, ensuring there is no pressure on the nostril rim to avoid skin damage.\n# 8. Confirmation: Aspiration\nI am attaching a 60ml syringe to aspirate content.\nI'm testing this on blue litmus paper.\nThe paper has turned red, confirming the content is acidic\n[PH < 5.5]\nand the tube is in the stomach.\n# 9. Confirmation: Whoosh Test\nI am rapidly injecting 20-30ml of air while listening over the epigastrium with my stethoscope...\nI hear a bubbling 'whoosh' sound.\n# 10. Conclusion & Documentation\nThank you, you can relax now.\nThe gold standard for confirmation is a\nChest X-ray, which I would order before starting any feeds.\nI will now document the procedure, noting the tube size, nostril used, and pH result.", "id": "b18", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Shoulder", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I am a medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and date of birth?\nNice to meet you\nI need to examine, so look at, feel and move, your shoulders today.\nIs that okay?\n[I am now washing my hands.]\nBefore we start, do you feel pain anywhere?\n# Look (Inspection)\nPlease remove your top so I can see both shoulders and your back clearly\n[Observe pt while they do so]\nInspecting both shoulders from front, side, and behind: Shoulder contours are symmetrical with no obvious abnormalities, wasting, scars or redness.\n# Wall Push-Up Test: Winging of the scapula\nPlease stand facing the wall, place both hands flat and perform a push-up against the wall.\nI do not see any winging of the scapula.\n# Feel/Palpation\nYou may take a seat\n[Stand behind/to the side]\nI am feeling for tenderness, bogginess, deformities, swelling or osteophytes and temperature.\nStarting at the sternoclavicular joint, I will feel across the clavicle to the acromioclavicular joint.\nI will move inferomedially to feel for the coracoid process, joint space, head of the humerus, and then along the spine of the scapula and inferior angle.\n[Repeat on the other side]\nWas there any pain?\n# Apley Scratch Test\nNow we'll do a general movement screening test: Imagine there is an itch on your back.\nScratch it reaching over your shoulder, across your neck and finally around your back.\n[Repeat on the other side]\nI am not seeing any signs of pain or limited range of motion, and both sides have equal reach.\n# Move\nYou may stand\" I will now test some active movements.\nFlexion: Please stretch your arms up to the ceiling\nExtension: …and down as far as you can go.\nWith one side at a time, move your arm out to 90 degrees\n[Abduction]\nand then all the way up\n[Elevation]\n, and finally down across your body\n[Adduction]\nDo the same with the other arm… Put your elbows to your side and twist them out as far as you go\n[Ext rotation]\n, and now with the thumb of one arm, bring it as high up on your back as you can go…repeat with the other arm\n[Internal rotation]\nDid you experience any pain?\nPatient has good range of motion in both shoulders on active movement with no pain.\n# Apley Scarf Test/Cross arm adductor test\nWe will start with Apley's Scarf Test.\nPlace the hand of your affected arm on your opposite shoulder, like a scarf.\nI am going to push your elbow gently across your body towards your chest and feel on the AC joint.\nDo you feel pain?\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Speed's Test\nNow for Speed's Test, fully straighten your elbow with your palm facing the ceiling.\nRaise your arm forward and push up against my hand while I feel the front of your shoulder for biceps tendinitis.\nDo you feel pain?\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Rotator Cuff:\nNow to test the rotator cuff muscles, starting with Hawkin's Kennedy Test for supraspinatus impingement.\nI am supporting your arm: Flex your shoulder to 90° and elbow to 90°.\nI am now passively internally rotating your shoulder by bringing your forearm downward...\nlet me know if this causes pain.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Jobe's / Soda Pop Test\nNow for the Soda Pop test for supraspinatus muscle:\nBring both arms out 90° to the side and 30° forward.\nTurn your hands thumbs-down as if emptying a soda can.\nHold them there and resist as I press down on your arms.\nIs there pain?\nNo signs of pain or weakness of supraspinatus\n# Infraspinatus Test\nNow to test your infraspinatus muscle.\nKeep your elbows tucked into your sides flexed at 90°.\nPush your forearms outwards against my hands while I resist you, don't let me push them in.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or weakness\n# Gerber's Lift-Off Test\nFinally we will perform the lift-off test for the subscapularis muscle.\nPlace the back of your hand against your lower back with the palm facing outward.\nNow try to lift your hand away from your spine against my resistance.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or weakness\n# Apprehension Test\nLift your arm and flex your elbow.\nI am going to externally rotate the arm while pushing on the head of the humerus, looking for any resistance or pain.\n# Passive Movement\nFinally, I will be moving your shoulder joint to assess passive movement.\nLet me know if there is any pain.\nStarting at one arm: Flexion\n[Upwards]\n, extension\n[Down and behind]\nabduction\n[Outwards]\n, and upwards beyond 90 degrees\n[Elevation]\n, adduction\n[Back down and across the body]\nNow with the elbow bent, rotate out\n[External rotation]\n, and behind the back\n[Internal rotation.]\nThere is no crepitus, instability or stiffness.\nThere was a good range of motion and no pain.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Close\nOn looking, feeling and moving your shoulder, I didn't detect any abnormalities or rotator cuff injuries.\nThank you so much, we are done.", "id": "b19", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Elbow", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, I am {me}, a medical student.\nMay I confirm your details?\nI am going to look at, feel and move your elbows today.\nIs that okay?\nPlease ensure your arms are exposed up to the mid-upper arm.\nAre you in any pain?\nLet's begin, I am washing my hands.\n# Look/Inspection\nPut your arms at your sides please.\nFirst, I will inspect the carrying angle.\nThe humerus comes down and the hands move about 5-15 degrees away from the legs, which is normal.\nI am noting any obvious deformities, wasting of the biceps and triceps on each side, obvious scarring, redness, swelling or bruising at the elbows.\n# Feel/Palpation\nYou may take a seat\" → Stand in front/to the side\nI am now going to feel at the head of the radius, the joint space and the lateral epicondyle, where tenderness would indicate tennis elbow.\nI can move across and feel the capitulum of the humerus, the olecranon of the ulna, the ulnar nerve and the medial epicondyle, where tenderness would indicate golfer's elbow.\nAny pain?\nNo evidence of pain, tenderness, swelling, bogginess, deformity or osteophytes/osteoporosis, Temperature is normal.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Move\nPlease stand and just copy my movements.\nFlex/bend your arms at the elbow, extend them.\nBend them to 90 degrees and turn your hands over for supination, and back again for pronatation.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or limitations on active movement.\n# Passive movement\nPlease allow me to move your arm while holding your elbow…starting with flexion up and extension.\nPlease hold my hand like we're shaking hands, I am going to move your arm into supination…and pronation.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or crepitus and good range of motion on passive movement.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Medial Epicondylitis Test (Golfer's Elbow)\nBend your arm at the elbow and cock your wrist with your palm up in a fist.\nResist while I pull down…Any pain in your elbow?\nNo pain at the lateral epicondyle.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Lateral Epicondylitis Test (Tennis Elbow)\nBend your arm at the elbow, I'll just hold your forearm.\nCock your wrist up with your palm facing down in a fist and try to resist as I push down…Any pain on your elbow?\nNo pain at the lateral epicondyle.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Close\nAfter looking at, feeling and moving your elbow joint, I didn't notice any abnormalities.\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b20", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Hand & wrist", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me}.\nMay I confirm your name?\nI need to examine, look at, move and feel your hands and wrists today.\nIs that okay?\nWe can begin, I am washing my hands, you can take a seat.\n[Sit next to pt on bed]\n# Look (Nodes & Deformities)\nPlease place both hands flat on this pillow.\nAre you feeling any pain?\nI am looking for any deformities, wasting, weakness, redness, scars, swelling.\nSpecifically, I will look at the distal interphalangeal joints for Heberden's Nodes, and proximal joints for Bouchard's nodes for osteoarthritis.\nI'm looking for swan neck and Boutonnière deformities at the fingers, ulnar deviation of the joints and Z deformity of the thumb.\nLooking for wasting, I'm focusing on the thenar compartment for median nerve compression and the hypothenar compartment for ulnar nerve compression.\nPlease hold your hands up, palms down. | WRIST DROP\nI am not seeing any signs of wrist drop or radial nerve injury.\nMake two fists for me, I'm looking at the transverse arches which appear normal and symmetrical.\nOpen your fingers please.\nI am looking at the longitudinal arches of each finger, which appear normal and symmetrical.\n# Feel\nI am now going to feel the wrist joints for increased temperature, tenderness, swelling, bogginess or deformity.\nPlease put your hands out…I will start by feeling the distal interphalangeal joints of both hands, then the proximal interphalangeal joints of all fingers and both thumbs\nNow I'm going to feel the metacarpophalangeal joints of both hands.\nI can now check for instability of the MP joints.\nPlease bend your fingers to 90 degrees.\nI'm shunting each one up and down to check for undue movement.\nI can also check for hyperlaxity.\nPlease relax your fingers, palms down…I am pushing them up to check if they bend past 90 degrees.\n[NB: BOTH HANDS]\nI'm squeezing the metatarsals and feeling the metacarpal-carpal joints, and the carpoulnar joint and carporadial joint.\nAny pain?\nNow I'm feeling the head of the ulna for tenderness of flexor carpi ulnaris.\nPlease lift your thumb.\nI am feeling the anatomical snuff box for tenderness, which may indicate a scaphoid fracture.\nI can now move to the radial styloid- Please relax your thumb- and feel tenderness due to extensor pollicis brevis and adductor pollicis longus tendinitis.\nREPEAT \"Finally I'll check the radial pulse for rate and rhythm\n# Carpal Tunnel Tests\nI am lightly tapping over the front of your wrist. | Tinel's Tap Test\nLet me know if there is any pain or tingling\" → Repeat Median Nerve Compression Test:\nI am applying firm pressure over the center of your wrist for 40 seconds.\nAny pain or tingling?\n→ Repeat Phalen's Test: Hold the backs of your hands together with wrists flexed at 90° for 60 seconds like this…Any pain or tingling?\n# Finkelstein's Test\nNow I will perform Finkelstein's test for De Quervain's Tenosinuvitis.\nTuck your thumb inside your palm and wrap your fingers over it to make a fist and relax it.\nI will now ulnar deviate the wrist…Any pain?\n# Move WRIST\nHold your hands out and make a fist.\nExtend your hands back…flex them forwards, shift them out for ulnar deviation and in for radial deviation…Turn them over for supination, and back for pronation.\nTo check flexion and extension again, press your hands together\n[Knuckles facing]\nfor flexion…and make a prayer sign for extension\" \"Any pain?\nPatient has a good, full range of movement without pain.\n# Passive movements\nRelax your hands so I can move them…Extension, flexion, ulnar deviation, radial deviation, pronation and supination.\nAny pain?\nREPEAT \"Normal symmetrical range of motion without crepitus or pain.\n# Move FINGERS\nMake a fist and gently open it to check flexion and extension.\nNow spread your fingers out\n[Abduction]\nand close them together\n[Adduction]\nHold your hands out palm up and bring your thumbs in and across\n[Flexion]\nand out\n[Extension]\nBring your thumbs up to the ceiling\n[Abduction]\nand down the palm\n[Adduction]\n, and touch your pinky finger\n[Opposition]\nAny pain?\n→ Full range of motion without pain on active movement.\n# Passive movement\nStarting with the fingers, I am going to flex, extend, adduct and abduct each one individually on both hands…And then flex, extend, adduct and abduct the thumb.\nAny pain?\nNo pain, crepitus or stiffness with a good full range of motion.\n# Tendon Tests (FDP & FDS)\nFlexor Digitorum Profundus\n[FDP]\n: \"I am holding your finger joint straight; bend just the tip of your finger.\nWe will do this for all fingers in isolation.\nFDP intact\nFlexor Digitorum Superficialis\n[FDS]\n: \"I am holding all your other fingers flat; bend your finger at the middle joint…for all fingers in isolation.\nFDS intact\n# Functionality Testing\nWe will now perform some quick function tests\" 1.\nPick up an Object: Pick up this small coin off the table with one hand…then the other.\nPower Grip: Squeeze two of my fingers as tightly as you can while I try to pull them out.\nGood grip strength3.\nPinch Grip: Pinch your thumb and index finger together tightly and squeeze my fingers.\nGood symmetrical grip strength.\n# Nerve Lesion Posture Assessment\nNow we will check for peripheral nerve injury\nChecking for\nWrist Drop: Hold your hand out\n[In pronation]\nand make a fist with your hand in extension.\nDon't let me pull them straight…Adequate power and no evidence of radial nerve fallout\nHand of Benediction: Put your hands out, palms up.\nMove your thumbs towards the ceiling and don't let me push them down…Adequate power and no evidence of median nerve fallout\nChecking for\nClaw Hand: Spread your fingers out and don't let me push them in…Adequate power and no evidence of Ulnar nerve fallout.\n# Froment's Sign\nWe can double check the ulnar nerve using\nFroment's test.\nHold this piece of paper tightly between your thumb and the side of your index finger using a flat pinch.\nHold on tightly and do not let me pull it away.\nThe patient should only adduct the thumb.\nIf the thumb flexes, the medium nerve is being used indicating ulnar nerve weakness.\n# Close\nI did not find any abnormalities in your wrist and fingers.\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b21", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Hip", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I am a medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI need to examine your hips and legs today.\nIs that okay?\n[Wash hands]\nGAIT\n# Trendelenburg's Test\nWe will start with Trendelenburg's Test.\nPlease stand up straight facing away from me.\nI am going to just place my hands gently over your hips\n[Crouch behind pt]\nPlease stand on one leg…I am checking that the pelvis stays horizontal and does not sag to the unsupported side.\nPlease stand on the other leg… If the pelvis does drop, that could indicate superior gluteal nerve injury affecting the hip adductors.\n# Leg Length Measurement\nWhile you are lying down, I'm going to measure leg length:\nTrue Leg Length: Measuring from ASIS to medial malleolus in both legs, which should be equal.\nApparent Leg Length: Measuring from umbilicus to medial malleolus in both legs, which should be even.\n# Feel/Palpate\nPlease lie down.\nAnd remove your shirt.\nI am palpating at the mid-inguinal point to feel for hip joint tenderness, as well as the ASIS\n[Thumbs]\nand greater trochanter of the femur\n[Fingers]\nto feel for tenderness, temperature change, and wasting.\nAny pain?\n# Move\nStay lying down and please flex your leg up at the hip joint as far as you can, bending your knee when needed.\nAt 90 degrees, keep your knee bent and bend your leg outwards\n[External rotation]\nand inwards\n[Internal rotation]\n, before lowering it.\nMove your leg outwards to the side as far as possible\n[Abduction]\nand back across\n[Adduction]\n[Repeat on the other side]\nDo passive movements, stabilising at hip \"Please roll onto your stomach…now lift your whole leg up…and the other leg to check for extension.\nAny pain?\nDo passive movements, stabilising at hip \"Good full range of movement at the hip without any pain on active movement, and no pain or crepitus on passive movement\n# Thomas Test\nI am looking for fixed flexion deformity of the hip using the Thomas test: I will place one hand behind the thoracolumbar spine to stabilise it.\nBend both knees up to your chest, and now lower one only…lift it up again.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# FABER Test (Patrick's Test)\nPlace your right ankle across your left knee in a 'figure-4' position\n[Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation]\nI press down gently on your flexed knee while stabilizing your opposite hip...\nwhere do you feel pain?\n# Close\nOn examination of your hip, I did not find any abnormalities.\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b22", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Knee", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, I am {me}, a medical student.\nMay I confirm your details?\nI am going to examine your knees today.\nIs that okay?\nGAIT: Smooth, symmetrical, normal posture, balanced, fluid turning Please lie flat on the couch with your legs fully exposed from mid-thigh down.\nAre you in any pain?\n[I am washing my hands.]\n# Look/Inspection\nLooking at the knees, first from the front.\nI am looking for any scars, swelling, patella facing forward, redness, bruising.\nInspecting alignment\n[Genu varum/valgum]\nfor knocked knees or bow-leggedness, swelling, peripatellar grooves, and muscle bulk\n[Vastus medialis]\nLooking from the side, I am looking that the knees aren't held in flexion or hyperextended.\nLooking at the back at the popliteal spaces, I am not seeing any swelling or abnormalities.\n# Feel/Palpation.\nPlease lie down.\nLet me know if you experience any pain as I feel your knee for temperature, tenderness, bogginess....\nI will start feeling the patella and checking that it is mobile.\nI will feel the patella tendon laterally, the tibial tuberosity for tenderness.\nI will feel along the joint line for osteophytes between the femur and tibia, around to the back and then feel in the popliteal fossa for any masses…and feel the popliteal pulse.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Milk/Fluid Test\nTo further check the patella, I will gently push distally/milk the fluid down from above the patella and press down to see if the patella sinks down to tap against the femur, indicating a distal effusion.\nI can then perform the\nBulge test by milking down and then pushing medially to look for a bulge of fluid on the medial side, which I should be able to push down to the other side.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Patella Apprehension Test\nLet me know if you feel any pain.\nI will also look at the patient's face for pain or apprehension.\nUsing my thumb, I will push the patella laterally and slowly lift the knee to 90 degrees while watching the face\n# Move\nI will now assess active movement.\nPlease bend your knee and try to bring your heel to your bum…now straighten it out.\nAny pain?\nREPEAT \"Full ROA of knee on active movement.\nRelax, I will now move you knee into full flexion…and extension.\nDuring extension, I can pause and hold onto the ankle, and observe the knee for hypertension.\nAny pain?\nREPEAT \"Full ROA without crepitus on passive movement.\n# Collateral Ligament Tests\nI will now begin examining your ligaments.\nStarting with the collateral ligaments.\nFlex your knee slightly to 15°–30°:\nValgus Stress: Holding your ankle and pushing medially against the outer knee.\nAny pain?\nI am also looking for undue movement of the medial collateral ligament.\nVarus Stress: Holding your ankle and pushing laterally against the inner knee.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or undue movement of the lateral collateral ligament\n# Sag Sign (PCL)\nNow I will examine the anterior and posterior cruciate ligaments.\nLie flat on your back with hips flexed to 45° and knees flexed to 90°, with feet flat on the bed.\nI am looking at the profile of your shins from the side for a sag or PCL tear.\n# Drawer Tests (ACL & PCL)\nIn the same position\n[Knees at 90°]\n, I will sit gently on your foot to stabilize it.\nI will wrap my hands around your upper shin\n[Thumbs at tibial tuberosity and fingers on calf muscles]\n: Let me know if there is any pain\nAnterior Drawer: Pulling tibia forward towards me.\nPosterior Drawer: Pushing tibia backward away from me.\nNo pain or undue movement.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# McMurray's Test\nNow I will test the Meniscus using McMurray's Test.\nFlexing knee and hip to 90° while holding your heel and feeling the joint line:\nMedial Meniscus: Externally rotate foot and apply valgus stress inwards while extending knee down slowly.\nI am looking for locking, clicking and pain.\nLateral Meniscus: Internally rotate foot and apply varus/outward stress while extending knee slowly.\nI am looking for locking, clicking and any pain.\n# Apley's Grinding & Distraction Tests\nPlease turn onto your stomach\n[Prone position]\nand flex your knee to 90°:\nGrinding Test: I will press down through your heel\n[Compression]\nwhile rotating shin inward and outward.\nAny pain?\nPain would indicate a meniscus injury.\nDistraction Test: Fixing your thigh using my knee, I will pull my leg upward\n[Distraction]\n, and rotate the shin.\nAny pain?\nPain would indicate a ligament injury, and relief would indicate a meniscus injury.\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Close\nI did not find any abnormalities at your knee joint or injury to the ligaments and meniscii.\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b23", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Foot & ankle", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, I am {me}.\nMay I confirm your name?\nI am going to examine your feet and ankles today.\nGAIT - Steady, symmetrical, smooth, fluid turning, posture, balance\nPlease lie flat with your feet extending over the end of the bed.\nAre you in any pain?\n[I am washing my hands.]\n# Look\nI am looking for wasting of the calf muscles, scars, swelling, deformities such as hallux valgus of the big toe, clawing or crowding of the toes.\nLooking at the arches, I am checking the longitudinal arch from the calcaneus to the ankle, then under the foot at the transverse arch for pes planus or flat feet as well as the carpal bones.\n# Feel\nI am now going to palpate down the calves for any wasting, along the achilles tendon of each foot for any gaps or nodules and along the calcaneus for any tenderness, and underneath for plantar fasciitis.\nI will also feel the medial and lateral malleoli as well as all the joints for tenderness.\nI am squeezing the metatarsals to check for early RA and then feeling every toe joint MP and IP on both sides.\nI am checking temperature, tenderness, boginess, swelling or deformities.\n# Move\nNow I will assess active movement.\nPlease bend your feet up as far as possible for dorsiflexion…and down as far as possible for plantarflexion.\nTwist your feet in for inversion and out for eversion.\nCurl your toes for flexion and spread them out for extension.\nAny pain?\nGood ROA without pain on active movement.\n# Passive movement\nRelax and let me move your feet.\nAt subtibial joint: Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion of both feet.\nAt subtalar joint: Inversion and eversion of both feet.\nAt midtarsal joint: Any pain on rotation for pronation and supination.\nAnd finally flexion and extension at each toe.\nAny pain?\nNo pain or crepitus with good ROA in all joints.\n# Simmonds' Test (Thompson Test)\nFinally I will perform Simmond's test.\nPlease kneel on the bed\n[Or lie on your stomach]\nwith your feet hanging off the edge.\nI am gently squeezing your calf/gastrocnemius muscle to check your Achilles tendon.\n# Close\nOn examination of your ankles and feet, I didn't find anything abnormal.\nAny questions?\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b24", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Cervical spine", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I am a medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and date of birth?\nI need to perform an examination of your neck and cervical spine today.\nPlease remove your shirt so your neck and upper shoulders are fully exposed.\nAre you currently feeling any pain anywhere?\nWe can begin.\nTake a seat while I wash my hands.\n# Look (Inspection)\nInspecting your neck and cervical spine from the front, side, and behind:\nPosture & Alignment: Checking for normal cervical lordosis.\nMuscles: Inspecting sternocleidomastoid\n[SCM]\n, trapezius, and nuchal paraspinal muscles for symmetry or wasting.\nSkin: Checking for surgical scars, rashes, or soft tissue swellings, redness or bruising.\n# Feel (Palpation)\nI am palpating/feeling the neck.\nLet me know if you feel any pain.\nBony Landmarks: Palpating spinous processes from C1 down to C7\n[Prominent bony landmark at the base of the neck]\nand facet joints 1 fingerbreadth laterally for steps, gaps, or tenderness, bogginess, increased temp down the midline…then I will palpate along each side for the facet joints..\nSoft Tissues: Palpating SCM, trapezius, and erector spinae muscles for spasm or tenderness.\n# Move (Active Range of Motion)\nTesting active range of motion of the cervical spine:\nFlexion\n[45°]\n: Touch your chin to your chest.\nExtension\n[45°]\n: Look directly up at the ceiling.\nLateral Rotation\n[70°]\n: Turn your head to look over your right shoulder, then your left.\nLateral Flexion\n[45°]\n: Touch your ear to your shoulder without raising your shoulder.\n# Passive movements\nPlease lie down and relax your neck, I'm going to be moving it, let me know if there is any pain.\nFlexion-Extension-Lateral Rotation, Lateral flexion.\nAny pain?\nThe patient has a good full range of motion without crepitus or pain.\n# Special Tests: Nerve Root Compression\nAxial Compression: Sit up and look forward I am applying gentle downward axial pressure on the top of your head.\nDoes this make the pain worse?\nPlacing my hands under your chin and occiput, I am gently applying upward traction and lifting. | Cervical Distraction Test\nDoes this relieve the pain?", "id": "b25", "builtin": true}, {"name": "MSK: Thoracolumbar spine", "lang": "en-ZA", "group": "MSK", "text": "# WIPER Setup & Prep\nGood day, my name is {me} and I am a medical student.\nMay I confirm your name and age?\nI need to perform an examination of your main back and spine today.\nPlease remove your shirt so your entire back is exposed from your neck down to your hips.\nAre you in any pain anywhere?\nPlease remain standing, I'm going to wash my hands….\n# Look (Inspection)\nStarting with inspection, I'm looking from the side for normal cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis and lumbar lordosis.\nThen I'll look from the back to check for scoliosis or any scars, swelling, bruising, redness or skin tufts\" \"Looking at the muscles, I'm not seeing any muscle wasting or spasm.\n# Feel (Palpation)\nNow I will feel over the spine.\nLet me know if there is any pain.\nPalpating down the spinous processes from T1 down to L5 or the sacrum for alignment, gaps, step-offs, or tenderness, bogginess, swelling, temperature.\nI am also going to feel down either side.\nPalpating paraspinal muscles bilaterally for muscle spasm.\nI will now press firmly with palms over both Sacroiliac\njoints beneath the PSIS dimples for tenderness.\n# Move (Range of Motion)\nTesting active thoracolumbar range of motion:\nFlexion & Extension: Keep your knees straight, bend forward to reach toward your toes, then lean backwards as far as you can.\nLateral Flexion\n[Lumbar]\n: Slide your hand down the outer side of your thigh…right and then left.\nI will also check that the hand reaches the same point on either side.\nRotation\n[Thoracic]\n: Sitting on the bed with pelvis fixed, twist your shoulders to look behind you to the right, then to the left.\nI am also checking that the elbows reach the same point.\n# Schober's Test (Lumbar Mobility)\nI will now perform Schober's test to check lumbar spine mobility.\nStanding erect, I am making a mark at L5\n[Level of the PSIS dimples]\nand a second mark 10 cm directly above it and 5cm below it.\nNow please bend forward to touch your toes as far as you can while I measure the distance between the two marks.\nIt should increase by at least 5cm/to 20cm.\n# FABER Test (Sacroiliac Joint)\nPlease lie flat on your back.\nPlace your right ankle across your left knee in a 'figure-4' position\n[Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation]\nI am placing one hand on your opposite hip to stabilise the pelvis and gently pressing down on your flexed knee...\ndo you feel pain in your groin or your back?\n[Repeat on the other side]\n# Sciatic Nerve Irritation Tests (L4–S1)\nStraight Leg Raise\n[SLR]\nLying flat on your back, I will hold your heel and slowly lift your leg upward while watching your face for pain. | Test\nTell me when you feel pain.\nAt 90 degrees, I can perform the Lasegue's Test: \"When pain is felt, I will lower your leg slightly until the pain stops, then passively bend your foot upward\n[Dorsiflexion]\nto stretch the sciatic nerve.\nAny pain or tingling?\nREPEAT No evidence of sciatic nerve root compression\n# Femoral Nerve Irritation Test (L2–L4)\nPlease turn onto your stomach\n[Prone position]\nI flex your knee to 90° and I will gently lift your thigh off the bed to extend your hip...\ndo you feel pain or tingling in the front of your thigh?\nREPEAT \"No evidence of femoral nerve root compression.\n# Close\nOn examination of the thoracolumbar spine, there is no evidence of abnormality or nerve root compression.\nThank you, we are done.", "id": "b26", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: Blood pressure", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Greeting And Introduction\n[Wash hands while greeting]\nSawubona Baba/Ma. | Hello Baba/Ma.\nUnjani? | How are you?\nNgubani igama lakho? | What is your name?\nNqiyajabula ukukwazi | Nice to meet you\nIgama lami ngu ____ . | My name is ____\nNgifundela ubudokotela lapha eWits | I am studying medicine here at Wits\n# Consent And Positioning\nNgidinga ukuhlola i-blood pressure | I need to examine your blood pressure\nNgingaqhubeka na? | May I continue?\nNgicela uhlale phansi. | Please sit down\nBeka ingalo lapha | Place your arm here\nPhakamisa ihembe lapha | Lift your shirt/sleeve\nUyangizwa na | Do you understand\n# Procedure And Instructions\nNgizoqinisa lento kwingalo | I will tighten this thing around your arm\nNgicela ukululeke | Please relax\nAkuzoba buhlungu | It will not be sore\n[Continue in English: Measure cuff → Place cuff → Estimated systolic → BP Reading]\n# Results And Conclusion\nI-blood pressure ilungile | The blood pressure is alright/normal (or: Iphezulu = High, iphanis = Low)\nUnemibuzo? | Any questions?\nSiqedile Baba/Ma ____ . | We are finished Baba/Ma\nNgiyabonga. | Thank you\nHamba kahle | Go well", "id": "b27", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: Urine dipstick", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Greeting And Introduction\n[Wash hands while greeting]\nSawubona Baba/Ma. | Hello Baba/Ma.\nUnjani? | How are you?\nNgubani igama lakho? | What is your name?\nNqiyajabula ukukwazi | Nice to meet you\nIgama lami ngu ____ . | My name is ____\nNgifundela ubudokotela lapha eWits | I am studying medicine here at Wits\n# Consent And Instructions\nNgicela ukuhlola umchamo. | I need to test your urine\nUyavuma na? | Do you agree?\nUzochama ethoyileti kuqala kancane, Uchame lapha Uqede ethoyilethe futhi | Urinate a little bit into the toilet, Then pee into here Then finish urinating into the toilet again.\n[Point to cup]\nUyangizwa na? | Do you understand?\nUnemibuzo | Any questions\n[Continue in English: Gloves → Check tube expiration → Hold sample 10 sec submerged in cup → Read top-down → Report]\n# Results And Conclusion\nSesiqedile | We are done\nUnemibuzo? | Any questions?\nNgiyabonga | Thank you\nHambakahle | Farewell", "id": "b28", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: Venepuncture", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Greeting And Introduction\n[Wash hands while greeting]\nSawubona Baba/Ma. | Hello Baba/Ma.\nUnjani? | How are you?\nNgubani igama lakho? | What is your name?\nNqiyajabula ukukwazi | Nice to meet you\nIgama lami ngu ____ . | My name is ____\nNgifundela ubudokotela lapha eWits | I am studying medicine here at Wits\n# Consent And Positioning\nNgifuna ukuthatha igazi manje. | I want to take your blood now\nNgingaqhubeka na? | May I continue?\nYelula lengalo lapha | Extend your arm here\nPhakamisa ihembe lapha | Lift your shirt/sleeve here\n# Tourniquet And Finding A Vein\nNgizoqinisa lento kwingalo | I will tighten this thing on the arm\nYenza inqindi | Make a fist\n[Put gloves on]\nNgizama ukuthola umthambo nje | I am just trying to find a vein\n# Cleaning Site & Needle Insertion\n[Check equipment (Needle, tubes, alcohol, cotton, tape)]\nNgisula isikhumba nje | I am just wiping the skin\nNgizohlaba ngenalithi | I am going to puncture with the needle\nKuzoba buhlungu kancane | It will be a little sore\n[Continue in English: Insert needle → Draw blood gently → 1-2-3 Removal → Tubes (Invert and lable) → Check site → Dispose gloves]\n# Removal And Conclusion\nNgicela ucofa lapha | Please press here\nSiqedile. | We are done.\nNgiyabonga, hamba kahle | Thank you, farewell", "id": "b29", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: IM injection", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Greeting And Introduction\n[Wash hands while greeting]\nSawubona Baba/Ma. | Hello Baba/Ma.\nUnjani? | How are you?\nNgubani igama lakho? | What is your name?\nNqiyajabula ukukwazi | Nice to meet you\nIgama lami ngu ____ . | My name is ____\nNgifundela ubudokotela lapha eWits | I am studying medicine here at Wits\n# Consent And Positioning\nNgidinga ukufaka umuthi ngenalithi | I need to give you medication using a needle.\nNgingaqhubeka na? | May I continue?\nNgicela ukhumule ibhulukwe kancane lapha | Remove your pants until here\n[Prepare all equipment and put gloves on + Choose site: 7 Rights (Person, time, medication, dose, site, refusal, documentation)]\nNgisula isikhumba nje | I am just wiping the site\nNgizohlaba ngenalithi | I am going to puncture with the needle\nKuzoba buhlungu kancane | It will be a little sore\nNgicela ukululeke | Please relax\n[Continue in English: Drawing needle → Administering needle → Z track --90 degrees → Aspirate → Slow injection → Dispose --Clean → Wash]\n# Removal And Conclusion\nNgicela ucindezele lapha | Please press here\nSiqedile. | We are done.\nNgiyabonga, hamba kahle | Thank you, farewell", "id": "b30", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: IV cannulation", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Greeting And Introduction\n[Wash hands while greeting]\nSawubona Baba/Ma. | Hello Baba/Ma.\nUnjani? | How are you?\nNgubani igama lakho? | What is your name?\nNqiyajabula ukukwazi | Nice to meet you\nIgama lami ngu ____ . | My name is ____\nNgifundela ubudokotela lapha eWits | I am studying medicine here at Wits\n# Consent And Positioning\nNgidinga ukufaka i-Drip kwingalo | I need to put a drip into your arm\nNgingaqhubeka na? | May I continue?\nYelula lengalo lapha | Extend your arm here\nPhakamisa ihembe lapha | Lift your shirt/sleeve here\n# Procedure And Instructions\nNqizoqinisa lento kwingalo | I will tighten this thing on your arm\nYenza inqindi | Make a fist\n[Prepare all equipment and put gloves on]\nNgizama ukuthola umthambo nje | I am just looking for a vein\nNgisula isikhumba nje | I am just wiping the site\nNgizohlaba ngenalithi | I am going to puncture with the needle\nKuzoba buhlungu kancane | It will be a little sore\nNgicela ukululeke | Please relax\n[Continue in English: Insert bevel up at 20 degrees → Look for flashback → Advance catheter → Remove and dispose needle → Secure → Attach line → Secure]\n# Removal And Conclusion\nNgicela ucindezele lapha | Please press here\nSiqedile. | We are done.\nNgiyabonga, hamba kahle | Thank you, farewell", "id": "b31", "builtin": true}, {"name": "isiZulu: Escape sentences", "lang": "zu-ZA", "group": "isiZulu", "text": "# Escape sentences\nNgikhuluma kancane kuphela. | I speak a little bit only\nUxolo, ngisafunda ukukhuluma isiZulu. | Sorry, I am still learning to speak isiZulu\nUxolo, angikhulumi kahle isiZulu. | Sorry, I don't speak isiZulu well\nAngikhulumi kakhulu isiZulu. | I don't speak a lot of Zulu\nNgicela ukukhuluma isiNgisi manje. | I request to speak English now\nNgicela ukukhuluma isiNgisi nawe. | I request to speak English with you\nMusa ukukhuluma ngokushesha / Ungasheshi. | Don't speak fast / Don't hurry\nNgicela uphinde. | Please repeat\nNgicela ubhale phansi. | Please write it down\nUxolo, angikuzwa. | Sorry, I don't hear/understand you\nUthini ngesiNgisi?. | What are you saying in English?\nYini ... ngesiZulu?. | What is ... in Zulu?", "id": "b32", "builtin": true}];
